The jump-cache probe of indirect branches inline (M9 track,
docs/tracks/m9-tcg-aarch64.md). Every ret / call * / jmp * (and every jump
that leaves its page) ended its TB with a call to helper_lookup_tb_ptr(): a
register sync, cpu_get_tb_cpu_state(), curr_cflags(), the breakpoint check
and the jump-cache compare, ~70 host instructions plus the call, 13 % of the
vCPU thread's self time on 7-Zip and 5-7 % on the games. Now
translator_lookup_and_goto_ptr() emits the probe as TCG ops: the target
computes pc / cs_base / flags exactly as its cpu_get_tb_cpu_state() does
(i386: hflags, the eflags bits, the x87 / SSE fast-mode bits, CS base + eip),
the generic part hashes the pc, compares the entry, the TB's cs_base, flags
and cflags (against cpu->tcg_cflags), the breakpoint list and gdb single-step
into one mismatch word, branch-free (a brcond would spill every live temp at
the block end), and one goto_ptr takes either the TB's code or the epilogue
- the main loop's tb_lookup() is the miss path, exactly what the helper did
when it found nothing. Off with -accel tcg,inline-lookup=off (the helper,
as before); the helper is also kept when exec logging or nochain is on,
one-insn-per-tb, CF_NO_GOTO_PTR, a 32-bit host, the x86-64 target's CS64.
Tests: the DOS batteries and the XP guest stage, unchanged.

